<sup><b>1</b></sup> Why do the heathen rage, and the people imagine a vain thing?<br />
<sup><b>2</b></sup> The kings of the earth set themselves, and the rulers take counsel together, against the Lord, and against his anointed, <i>saying</i>,<br />
<sup><b>3</b></sup> Let us break their bands asunder, and cast away their cords from us.<br />
<sup><b>4</b></sup> He that sitteth in the heavens shall laugh: the Lord shall have them in derision.<br />
<sup><b>5</b></sup> Then shall he speak unto them in his wrath, and vex them in his sore displeasure.<br />
<sup><b>6</b></sup> Yet have I set my king upon my holy hill of Zion.<br />
<sup><b>7</b></sup> I will declare the decree: the Lord hath said unto me, Thou <i>art</i> my Son; this day have I begotten thee.<br />
<sup><b>8</b></sup> Ask of me, and I shall give <i>thee</i> the heathen <i>for</i> thine inheritance, and the uttermost parts of the earth <i>for</i> thy possession.<br />
<sup><b>9</b></sup> Thou shalt break them with a rod of iron; thou shalt dash them in pieces like a potter’s vessel.<br />
<sup><b>10</b></sup> Be wise now therefore, O ye kings: be instructed, ye judges of the earth.<br />
<sup><b>11</b></sup> Serve the Lord with fear, and rejoice with trembling.<br />
<sup><b>12</b></sup> Kiss the Son, lest he be angry, and ye perish <i>from</i> the way, when his wrath is kindled but a little. Blessed <i>are</i> all they that put their trust in him. <br />
